    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"1181248\">@Marco Krumpholz</span> Are you reading <em>How To Prove It with Lean</em>?  I notice that you used <code>assume</code> and <code>fix</code>, which are not standard Lean tactics but are defined in the package that comes with <em>How To Prove It with Lean</em>.  If that's what you're reading, take a look at Section 3.3, under the heading \"To use a given of the form <code>∀ (x : U), P x</code>\".  It explains that if you have <code>h : ∀ (x : U), P x</code> and <code>a : U</code>, then <code>h a</code> is a proof of <code>P a</code>.</p>",
        "id": 615908018,
        "sender_full_name": "Dan Velleman",
        "timestamp": 1786469307
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"453098\">@Dan Velleman</span> no we are using the Hitchhikers guide to logical verification. </p>\n<p>But I found the following the sentence which might have exactly the same meaning but different. </p>\n<p><code>∀x : σ, Q[x] can be read as “for all x, Q[x],” or as the type of functions of type (x : σ) → Q[x], mapping values x of type σ to proofs of Q[x].</code></p>",
